WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.066 --> 00:45.046 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 18,

00:45.396 --> 00:47.166 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:47.166 --> 00:47.476 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:47.476 --> 00:49.776 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:49.776 --> 00:53.346 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:53.346 --> 00:55.676 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:57.046 --> 00:58.516 align:middle
Our economy is strong overall

00:58.666 --> 01:01.096 align:middle
and has made significant
progress toward our goals

01:01.136 --> 01:02.446 align:middle
over the past two years.

01:03.296 --> 01:06.466 align:middle
The labor market has cooled from
its formerly overheated state.

01:07.556 --> 01:09.966 align:middle
Inflation has eased
substantially from a peak

01:09.966 --> 01:14.146 align:middle
of 7 percent to an estimated
2.2 percent as of August.

01:15.286 --> 01:17.616 align:middle
We're committed to maintaining
our economy's strength

01:17.906 --> 01:20.906 align:middle
by supporting maximum employment
and returning inflation

01:21.296 --> 01:22.376 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal.

01:24.376 --> 01:26.506 align:middle
Today, the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

01:26.506 --> 01:28.536 align:middle
to reduce the degree
of policy restraint

01:28.956 --> 01:32.376 align:middle
by lowering our policy interest
rate by ½ percentage point.

01:33.486 --> 01:36.166 align:middle
This decision reflects
our growing confidence

01:36.406 --> 01:38.236 align:middle
that with an appropriate
recalibration

01:38.236 --> 01:40.406 align:middle
of our policy stance, strength

01:40.406 --> 01:43.356 align:middle
in the labor market can
be maintained in a context

01:43.356 --> 01:46.536 align:middle
of moderate growth and
inflation moving sustainably

01:46.676 --> 01:47.576 align:middle
down to 2 percent.

01:49.086 --> 01:52.176 align:middle
We also decided to continue to
reduce our securities holdings.

01:52.176 --> 01:55.686 align:middle
I will have more to say
about monetary policy

01:55.686 --> 01:58.216 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

02:00.936 --> 02:03.936 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

02:03.936 --> 02:05.566 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

02:06.606 --> 02:10.056 align:middle
GDP rose at an annual rate of
2.2 percent in the first half

02:10.056 --> 02:13.816 align:middle
of the year, and available data
point to a roughly similar pace

02:13.816 --> 02:15.006 align:middle
of growth this quarter.

02:16.576 --> 02:18.896 align:middle
Growth of consumer spending
has remained resilient,

02:19.106 --> 02:21.476 align:middle
and investment in equipment
and intangibles has picked

02:21.476 --> 02:24.086 align:middle
up from its anemic
pace last year.

02:25.206 --> 02:27.236 align:middle
In the housing sector,
investment fell back

02:27.236 --> 02:29.966 align:middle
in the second quarter after
rising strongly in the first.

02:31.286 --> 02:34.416 align:middle
Improving supply conditions
have supported resilient demand

02:34.416 --> 02:35.546 align:middle
and the strong performance

02:35.546 --> 02:37.376 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
over the past year.

02:38.746 --> 02:40.586 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

02:40.776 --> 02:43.846 align:middle
Committee participants
generally expect GDP growth

02:43.846 --> 02:47.336 align:middle
to remain solid, with a
median projection of 2 percent

02:47.846 --> 02:49.036 align:middle
over the next few years.

02:50.806 --> 02:52.956 align:middle
In the labor market, conditions
have continued to cool.

02:53.816 --> 02:57.146 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 116,000 per month

02:57.146 --> 02:59.966 align:middle
over the past three
months, a notable step-down

02:59.966 --> 03:01.906 align:middle
from the pace seen
earlier in the year.

03:03.066 --> 03:04.496 align:middle
The unemployment
rate has moved up

03:04.696 --> 03:06.846 align:middle
but remains low at 4.2 percent.

03:08.366 --> 03:10.736 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

03:11.006 --> 03:13.046 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

03:14.196 --> 03:16.566 align:middle
Overall, a broad set
of indicators suggests

03:16.566 --> 03:19.056 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are now less tight

03:19.136 --> 03:21.906 align:middle
than just before the
pandemic in 2019.

03:23.316 --> 03:24.846 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

03:24.846 --> 03:26.936 align:middle
of elevated inflationary
pressures.

03:28.856 --> 03:30.696 align:middle
The median projection
for the unemployment rate

03:30.696 --> 03:34.096 align:middle
in the SEP is 4.4 percent
at the end of this year,

03:34.536 --> 03:36.526 align:middle
four-tenths higher
than projected in June.

03:38.256 --> 03:41.456 align:middle
Inflation has eased notably over
the past two years but remains

03:41.456 --> 03:43.896 align:middle
above our longer-run
goal of 2 percent.

03:44.916 --> 03:47.456 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

03:47.456 --> 03:48.626 align:middle
and other data indicate

03:48.876 --> 03:52.146 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.2 percent

03:52.626 --> 03:55.316 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in August and that,

03:55.316 --> 03:57.746 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:58.106 --> 04:01.076 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.7 percent.

04:02.746 --> 04:04.956 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

04:04.956 --> 04:08.186 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

04:08.186 --> 04:11.516 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters

04:12.006 --> 04:13.986 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

04:15.406 --> 04:16.816 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

04:17.036 --> 04:20.276 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
is 2.3 percent this year

04:20.776 --> 04:24.046 align:middle
and 2.1 percent next
year, somewhat lower

04:24.046 --> 04:25.226 align:middle
than projected in June.

04:26.266 --> 04:28.946 align:middle
Thereafter, the median
projection is 2 percent.

04:30.686 --> 04:34.996 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

04:34.996 --> 04:37.596 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:37.596 --> 04:38.626 align:middle
for the American people.

04:39.486 --> 04:42.286 align:middle
For much of the past three
years, inflation ran well

04:42.286 --> 04:43.696 align:middle
above our 2 percent goal,

04:44.416 --> 04:46.966 align:middle
and labor market conditions
were extremely tight.

04:48.096 --> 04:50.426 align:middle
Our primary focus
had been on bringing

04:50.426 --> 04:52.256 align:middle
down inflation, and
appropriately so.

04:53.326 --> 04:56.956 align:middle
We are acutely aware that high
inflation imposes significant

04:56.956 --> 04:59.476 align:middle
hardship, as it erodes
purchasing power,

04:59.916 --> 05:02.566 align:middle
especially for those least
able to meet the higher costs

05:02.566 --> 05:05.966 align:middle
of essentials like food,
housing, and transportation.

05:07.256 --> 05:10.126 align:middle
Our restrictive monetary policy
has helped restore the balance

05:10.166 --> 05:12.016 align:middle
between aggregate
supply and demand,

05:12.556 --> 05:14.976 align:middle
easing inflationary
pressures and ensuring

05:14.976 --> 05:17.526 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remain well anchored.

05:18.796 --> 05:21.726 align:middle
Our patient approach over the
past year has paid dividends:

05:22.586 --> 05:24.956 align:middle
Inflation is now much
closer to our objective,

05:25.566 --> 05:27.306 align:middle
and we have gained
greater confidence

05:27.306 --> 05:30.646 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

05:33.006 --> 05:35.586 align:middle
As inflation has declined and
the labor market has cooled,

05:35.726 --> 05:37.926 align:middle
the upside risks to
inflation have diminished,

05:38.296 --> 05:40.936 align:middle
and the downside risks to
employment have increased.

05:41.796 --> 05:44.196 align:middle
We now see the risks to
achieving our employment

05:44.196 --> 05:48.456 align:middle
and inflation goals as roughly
in balance, and we are attentive

05:48.456 --> 05:50.906 align:middle
to the risks to both
sides of our dual mandate.

05:52.616 --> 05:54.726 align:middle
In light of the progress on
inflation and the balance

05:54.726 --> 05:57.016 align:middle
of risks, at today's
meeting, the Committee decided

05:57.016 --> 05:59.276 align:middle
to lower the target range
for the federal funds rate

05:59.576 --> 06:04.146 align:middle
by ½ percentage point to
4  percent to 5 percent.

06:05.096 --> 06:08.646 align:middle
This recalibration of our policy
stance will help maintain the

06:08.646 --> 06:11.726 align:middle
strength of the economy and the
labor market and will continue

06:11.726 --> 06:14.056 align:middle
to enable further
progress on inflation

06:14.556 --> 06:15.746 align:middle
as we begin the process

06:15.796 --> 06:18.006 align:middle
of moving toward a
more neutral stance.

06:19.196 --> 06:21.536 align:middle
We are not on any preset course.

06:22.236 --> 06:24.956 align:middle
We will continue to make our
decisions meeting by meeting.

06:26.746 --> 06:29.626 align:middle
We know that reducing policy
restraint too quickly could

06:29.626 --> 06:31.166 align:middle
hinder progress on inflation.

06:32.376 --> 06:35.096 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
restraint too slowly could

06:35.096 --> 06:37.436 align:middle
unduly weaken economic
activity and employment.

06:38.826 --> 06:41.276 align:middle
In considering additional
adjustments to the target range

06:41.276 --> 06:42.246 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

06:42.646 --> 06:44.956 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess incoming data,

06:45.446 --> 06:47.596 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

06:49.336 --> 06:52.166 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

06:52.166 --> 06:53.666 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

06:53.666 --> 06:55.916 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

06:56.576 --> 06:58.516 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

06:58.516 --> 07:01.116 align:middle
to be the most likely
scenario going forward.

07:02.156 --> 07:03.926 align:middle
If the economy evolves
as expected,

07:04.196 --> 07:06.856 align:middle
the median participant projects
that the appropriate level

07:06.856 --> 07:10.146 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 4.4 percent at the end

07:10.146 --> 07:14.626 align:middle
of this year and 3.4
percent at the end of 2025.

07:16.206 --> 07:18.636 align:middle
These median projections
are lower than in June,

07:18.826 --> 07:21.136 align:middle
consistent with the
projections for lower inflation

07:21.136 --> 07:24.746 align:middle
and higher unemployment, as well
as the changed balance of risks.

07:25.936 --> 07:27.056 align:middle
These projections, however,

07:27.056 --> 07:28.996 align:middle
are not a Committee
plan or decision.

07:29.596 --> 07:33.176 align:middle
As the economy evolves, monetary
policy will adjust in order

07:33.176 --> 07:35.016 align:middle
to best promote our
maximum-employment

07:35.376 --> 07:36.776 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:37.856 --> 07:40.446 align:middle
If the economy remains solid
and inflation persists,

07:40.896 --> 07:43.176 align:middle
we can dial back policy
restraint more slowly.

07:43.176 --> 07:46.476 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

07:46.596 --> 07:49.086 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

07:49.436 --> 07:50.666 align:middle
we are prepared to respond.

07:51.536 --> 07:54.076 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

07:54.156 --> 07:55.506 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

07:55.896 --> 07:58.166 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

08:00.136 --> 08:01.726 align:middle
The Fed has been
assigned two goals

08:01.726 --> 08:05.836 align:middle
for monetary policy-maximum
employment and stable prices.

08:06.566 --> 08:08.936 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

08:09.366 --> 08:11.786 align:middle
bringing inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal,

08:12.126 --> 08:14.766 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

08:15.326 --> 08:15.876 align:middle
well anchored.

08:16.826 --> 08:18.016 align:middle
Our success in delivering

08:18.016 --> 08:19.986 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

08:20.586 --> 08:23.926 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

08:24.006 --> 08:25.626 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

08:26.296 --> 08:28.836 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

08:29.936 --> 08:31.566 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

08:31.676 --> 08:33.166 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

08:33.416 --> 08:34.586 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

08:35.346 --> 08:35.706 align:middle
Thank you.

08:35.986 --> 08:37.516 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

08:37.736 --> 08:38.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:39.566 --> 08:40.746 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

08:40.746 --> 08:41.946 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

08:41.946 --> 08:43.956 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking our questions.

08:43.956 --> 08:46.166 align:middle
In July, you said you weren't
necessarily thinking about a 50.

08:46.166 --> 08:47.156 align:middle
You didn't want to be specific,

08:47.196 --> 08:48.826 align:middle
but you said you weren't
thinking about a 50.

08:49.006 --> 08:50.856 align:middle
The inflation data
last week came

08:50.856 --> 08:52.266 align:middle
out a little firmer
than expected.

08:52.326 --> 08:55.866 align:middle
Retail was strong, third-quarter
GDP running 3 percent.

08:55.866 --> 08:59.036 align:middle
So what changed that
made the Committee go 50,

08:59.036 --> 09:00.616 align:middle
and how do you respond
to the concerns

09:00.616 --> 09:02.756 align:middle
that perhaps it shows
the Fed is more concerned

09:02.756 --> 09:03.826 align:middle
about the labor market?

09:04.366 --> 09:06.986 align:middle
And I guess, should we expect
more 50s in the months ahead?

09:07.436 --> 09:09.386 align:middle
And based on what
should we make that call?

09:09.496 --> 09:09.816 align:middle
Thank you.

09:10.126 --> 09:11.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:11.876 --> 09:14.396 align:middle
So, okay, a lot of
questions in there.

09:14.396 --> 09:17.866 align:middle
[Laughter] Let me jump in.

09:17.976 --> 09:20.316 align:middle
So, since the last meeting-okay,

09:20.316 --> 09:23.176 align:middle
the last meeting we have
had a lot of data come in.

09:23.176 --> 09:25.686 align:middle
We've had the two employment
reports, July and August.

09:26.186 --> 09:28.506 align:middle
We've also had two inflation
reports, including one

09:28.506 --> 09:29.936 align:middle
that came in during blackout.

09:30.676 --> 09:33.466 align:middle
We had the QCEW report,
which suggests

09:33.466 --> 09:35.426 align:middle
that maybe-that not
maybe, but suggests

09:35.426 --> 09:38.256 align:middle
that the payroll report numbers

09:38.256 --> 09:40.816 align:middle
that we're getting may
be artificially high

09:40.976 --> 09:42.636 align:middle
and will be revised down.

09:42.866 --> 09:43.336 align:middle
You know that.

09:44.076 --> 09:46.446 align:middle
We've also seen anecdotal data,

09:46.446 --> 09:47.376 align:middle
like [the data collected
in] the Beige Book.

09:47.476 --> 09:52.636 align:middle
So we took all of those,
and we went into blackout.

09:52.636 --> 09:55.406 align:middle
And we thought about what
to do, and we concluded

09:55.406 --> 09:58.236 align:middle
that this was the right thing
for the economy, for the people

09:58.236 --> 10:00.416 align:middle
that we serve, and that's,
that's how we made our decision.

10:01.096 --> 10:02.316 align:middle
So that's one question.

10:02.796 --> 10:04.776 align:middle
What was the second and third?

10:04.846 --> 10:05.456 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

10:05.456 --> 10:06.776 align:middle
How do we make the-sorry,
thank you, sir.

10:07.126 --> 10:09.536 align:middle
How do we figure out
in the months ahead,

10:09.536 --> 10:12.506 align:middle
is there another 25
or 50 coming in based

10:12.506 --> 10:13.686 align:middle
on what should we
make that call?

10:13.746 --> 10:14.066 align:middle
Thank you.

10:14.066 --> 10:14.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:15.256 --> 10:17.126 align:middle
Sure. So a couple things.

10:17.126 --> 10:22.096 align:middle
A good place to start is the-is
the SEP, but let me start

10:22.096 --> 10:24.106 align:middle
with what I said, which
was that we're going

10:24.106 --> 10:27.116 align:middle
to be making decisions
meeting by meeting based

10:27.116 --> 10:28.356 align:middle
on the incoming data,

10:28.406 --> 10:30.586 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
the balance of risks.

10:31.346 --> 10:35.456 align:middle
If you look at the SEP,
you'll, you'll see that it's,

10:35.756 --> 10:40.786 align:middle
it's a process of recalibrating
our policy stance away

10:40.786 --> 10:43.586 align:middle
from where we had it a year
ago, when inflation was high

10:43.586 --> 10:46.976 align:middle
and unemployment low, to a place
that's more appropriate given

10:46.976 --> 10:48.776 align:middle
where we are now and
where we expect to be.

10:48.776 --> 10:50.876 align:middle
And that process will
take place over time.

10:51.276 --> 10:53.136 align:middle
There's nothing in
the-in the SEP

10:53.136 --> 10:56.216 align:middle
that suggests the Committee
is in a rush to get this done.

10:56.216 --> 10:58.426 align:middle
This, this process
evolves over time.

10:58.676 --> 11:00.646 align:middle
Of course, that's a projection.

11:00.646 --> 11:02.116 align:middle
That's a baseline projection.

11:02.536 --> 11:06.226 align:middle
We know, as I mentioned in my
remarks, that the actual things

11:06.226 --> 11:08.306 align:middle
that we do will depend on
the way the economy evolves.

11:08.306 --> 11:10.036 align:middle
We can go quicker if
that's appropriate.

11:10.036 --> 11:11.616 align:middle
We can go slower if
that's appropriate.

11:11.616 --> 11:12.906 align:middle
We can pause if that's
appropriate.

11:13.356 --> 11:15.176 align:middle
But that's, that's what
we are contemplating.

11:15.266 --> 11:19.406 align:middle
Again, I would point you to
the SEP as just an assessment

11:19.406 --> 11:23.376 align:middle
of where-what the Committee
is thinking today-what the

11:23.376 --> 11:24.596 align:middle
individual members, rather,

11:24.596 --> 11:25.976 align:middle
of the Committee
are thinking today,

11:26.646 --> 11:30.506 align:middle
assuming that their particular
forecasts take-you know,

11:30.506 --> 11:31.096 align:middle
are realized.

11:33.556 --> 11:33.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:34.416 --> 11:36.366 align:middle
Let's go to Chris.

11:36.536 --> 11:37.256 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

11:37.256 --> 11:38.616 align:middle
Hi. Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

11:38.616 --> 11:39.066 align:middle
Thank you.

11:39.606 --> 11:43.086 align:middle
The projections show that the
Fed-Fed officials expect the fed

11:43.086 --> 11:45.546 align:middle
funds rate to still be
above their estimate

11:45.546 --> 11:47.606 align:middle
of long-run neutral by
the end of next year.

11:48.296 --> 11:51.806 align:middle
So does that suggest that
you see rates as restrictive

11:52.146 --> 11:53.606 align:middle
for that entire period?

11:53.606 --> 11:55.486 align:middle
Does that threaten the
weakening of the job market

11:55.486 --> 11:56.776 align:middle
that you said you'd
like to avoid?

11:57.406 --> 12:00.636 align:middle
Or does it suggest that maybe
people see the short-run neutral

12:00.636 --> 12:01.506 align:middle
as a little bit higher?

12:01.906 --> 12:02.896 align:middle
Thank you.

12:02.896 --> 12:03.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:03.426 --> 12:06.176 align:middle
I think it would-the way I would
really characterize it is this:

12:06.176 --> 12:09.166 align:middle
I think people write down
their estimate, individuals do.

12:09.526 --> 12:11.536 align:middle
I think every single
person on the Committee,

12:11.536 --> 12:14.026 align:middle
if you asked them, what's your
level of certainty around that,

12:14.026 --> 12:17.436 align:middle
they would say there's a wide
range where that could fall.

12:17.516 --> 12:19.136 align:middle
So I think we don't know.

12:19.136 --> 12:22.966 align:middle
There are model-based approaches
and empirically based approaches

12:22.966 --> 12:25.896 align:middle
that estimate what the neutral
rate will be at any given time.

12:26.406 --> 12:29.216 align:middle
But, realistically, we
know it by its works.

12:29.586 --> 12:32.816 align:middle
So that leaves us in a place
where we'll be-where we expect,

12:32.916 --> 12:34.936 align:middle
in the base case,
to be continuing

12:34.936 --> 12:37.546 align:middle
to remove restriction,
and we'll be looking

12:37.546 --> 12:39.486 align:middle
at the way the economy
reacts to that.

12:39.946 --> 12:42.936 align:middle
And that'll be guiding us in
our thinking about the question

12:42.936 --> 12:44.356 align:middle
that we're asking at
every meeting, which is,

12:44.406 --> 12:46.276 align:middle
is our policy stance
the appropriate one?

12:46.726 --> 12:48.926 align:middle
We know-if you go back, we know

12:48.926 --> 12:54.216 align:middle
that the policy stance we
adopted in July of 2023 came

12:54.576 --> 12:57.066 align:middle
at a time when unemployment
was 3½ percent

12:57.066 --> 12:58.826 align:middle
and inflation was 4.2 percent.

12:59.286 --> 13:02.886 align:middle
Today, unemployment
is up to 4.2 percent;

13:02.886 --> 13:06.286 align:middle
inflation's down to
a few tenths above 2.

13:06.706 --> 13:11.676 align:middle
So we know that it is time
to recalibrate our policy

13:12.376 --> 13:15.536 align:middle
to something that is more
appropriate given the progress

13:15.536 --> 13:18.726 align:middle
on inflation and on
employment moving

13:18.726 --> 13:20.806 align:middle
to a more sustainable level.

13:20.986 --> 13:23.096 align:middle
So the balance of
risks are now even.

13:23.096 --> 13:25.826 align:middle
And this is the beginning
of that process I mentioned,

13:25.826 --> 13:29.606 align:middle
the direction of which is
toward a sense of neutral,

13:29.856 --> 13:32.906 align:middle
and we'll move as
fast or as slow

13:32.906 --> 13:35.086 align:middle
as we think is appropriate
in real time.

13:35.156 --> 13:38.316 align:middle
What you have is our
individual-accumulation

13:38.316 --> 13:41.296 align:middle
of individual estimates of what
that will be in the base case.

13:43.016 --> 13:45.836 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:46.116 --> 13:47.436 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:47.436 --> 13:49.026 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

13:49.186 --> 13:51.706 align:middle
How close was this in
terms of the decision?

13:51.706 --> 13:55.606 align:middle
You do have the first dissent by
a Governor since 2005, I think.

13:56.076 --> 13:59.466 align:middle
Was the weight clearly
in favor of a 50,

13:59.466 --> 14:01.556 align:middle
or was this a very
close decision?

14:01.786 --> 14:02.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:02.266 --> 14:04.516 align:middle
I think we had a
good-a good discussion.

14:04.516 --> 14:09.006 align:middle
You know, if you go
back, I talked about this

14:09.006 --> 14:11.406 align:middle
at Jackson Hole, but I didn't
address the question of the size

14:11.406 --> 14:12.496 align:middle
of the cut and left it open,

14:12.496 --> 14:15.046 align:middle
and I think we left it
open going into blackout.

14:15.666 --> 14:18.726 align:middle
And so there was a lot of
discussion back and forth,

14:18.776 --> 14:21.096 align:middle
good diversity of-excellent
discussion today.

14:21.096 --> 14:23.606 align:middle
I think there was also broad
support for the decision

14:24.086 --> 14:25.626 align:middle
that the Committee voted on.

14:25.626 --> 14:28.366 align:middle
So I would add, though,
look at the SEP.

14:28.696 --> 14:32.946 align:middle
All 19 of the participants wrote
down multiple cuts this year.

14:33.286 --> 14:37.356 align:middle
All 19. That's a big
change from June, right?

14:37.646 --> 14:42.586 align:middle
Seven of the-seven of them wrote
down three or more and-sorry,

14:42.736 --> 14:45.006 align:middle
17 of the 19 wrote down
three or more cuts,

14:45.006 --> 14:47.366 align:middle
and 10 of the 19 wrote
down four or more cuts.

14:47.366 --> 14:50.176 align:middle
So, you know, there is a
dissent, and there's a range

14:50.176 --> 14:52.616 align:middle
of views, but there's actually
a lot of common ground as well.

14:53.146 --> 14:53.826 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

14:54.306 --> 14:55.926 align:middle
Follow-up to that.

14:55.926 --> 14:58.946 align:middle
Now that this is in the books,
can you give us some guidance

14:58.946 --> 15:01.316 align:middle
as sort of a follow-up to
Steve on the pacing here?

15:01.906 --> 15:03.046 align:middle
Would you expect this

15:03.106 --> 15:05.676 align:middle
to be running every
other meeting once we get

15:05.676 --> 15:06.546 align:middle
into next year?

15:07.596 --> 15:08.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:08.126 --> 15:10.446 align:middle
We're going to take it meeting
by meeting, as I mentioned.

15:10.576 --> 15:13.116 align:middle
There's no sense that
the Committee feels it's

15:13.116 --> 15:14.176 align:middle
in a rush to do this.

15:14.656 --> 15:17.266 align:middle
We made a good, strong start
to this, and that's really,

15:17.726 --> 15:20.496 align:middle
frankly, a sign of our
confidence-confidence

15:20.496 --> 15:24.416 align:middle
that inflation is, is
coming down toward 2 percent

15:24.416 --> 15:25.406 align:middle
on a sustainable basis.

15:25.406 --> 15:26.716 align:middle
That gives us the ability.

15:27.466 --> 15:29.886 align:middle
We can, you know, make
a good, strong start.

15:29.956 --> 15:32.966 align:middle
But-and I'm very
pleased that we did.

15:32.966 --> 15:35.496 align:middle
To me, the logic of this, both
from an economic standpoint

15:35.496 --> 15:37.616 align:middle
and also from a risk-management
standpoint, was clear.

15:38.296 --> 15:42.046 align:middle
But I think we're going to go
carefully, meeting by meeting,

15:42.946 --> 15:44.546 align:middle
and make our decisions as we go.

15:44.546 --> 15:44.856 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:45.376 --> 15:45.586 align:middle
Thank you.

15:45.936 --> 15:46.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:46.256 --> 15:47.826 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

15:50.266 --> 15:50.846 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

15:50.846 --> 15:52.076 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, New York Times.

15:52.076 --> 15:53.156 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

15:53.796 --> 15:56.936 align:middle
You and your colleagues in your
economic projections today see

15:56.936 --> 15:58.086 align:middle
the unemployment rate climbing

15:58.086 --> 16:00.266 align:middle
to 4.4 percent and
staying there.

16:00.656 --> 16:03.236 align:middle
Obviously, historically, when
the unemployment rate climbs

16:03.286 --> 16:05.306 align:middle
that much over a relatively
short period of time,

16:05.306 --> 16:07.916 align:middle
it doesn't typically just
stop-it continues increasing.

16:08.236 --> 16:09.356 align:middle
And so I wonder if
you can walk us

16:09.486 --> 16:12.096 align:middle
through why you see the
labor market stabilizing,

16:12.096 --> 16:13.526 align:middle
sort of what's the
mechanism there?

16:13.526 --> 16:15.866 align:middle
And what do you see
as the risks?

16:16.596 --> 16:16.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:17.266 --> 16:21.576 align:middle
So, again, the labor market is
actually in solid condition,

16:22.296 --> 16:25.536 align:middle
and our intention with
our policy move today is

16:25.536 --> 16:26.136 align:middle
to keep it there.

16:26.716 --> 16:28.136 align:middle
You can say that about
the whole economy.

16:28.136 --> 16:29.686 align:middle
The U.S. economy
is in good shape.

16:29.816 --> 16:34.026 align:middle
It's growing at a solid pace,
inflation is coming down,

16:34.376 --> 16:37.276 align:middle
the labor market is in a strong
pace, we want to keep it there.

16:37.706 --> 16:38.836 align:middle
That's, that's what we're doing.

16:38.836 --> 16:39.176 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:43.726 --> 16:44.086 align:middle
Sorry-Nick.

16:44.086 --> 16:50.066 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

16:50.686 --> 16:51.956 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

16:52.266 --> 16:55.116 align:middle
Chair Powell, does today's
action constitute a catch-up

16:55.246 --> 16:57.866 align:middle
in action given recent
substantial revisions

16:57.906 --> 16:58.986 align:middle
to the employment data,

16:59.606 --> 17:02.526 align:middle
or is this larger-than-typical
rate cut a function

17:02.576 --> 17:05.726 align:middle
of the elevated nominal
level of the policy rate

17:05.726 --> 17:10.186 align:middle
such that an accelerated cadence
could be expected to continue?

17:10.886 --> 17:11.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:11.636 --> 17:14.556 align:middle
Okay, multiple questions
in there.

17:14.556 --> 17:18.006 align:middle
So I would say we don't
think we're behind.

17:18.386 --> 17:20.166 align:middle
We do not think-we
think this is timely,

17:20.646 --> 17:22.646 align:middle
but I think you can
take this as a sign

17:22.956 --> 17:24.456 align:middle
of our commitment
not to get behind.

17:25.166 --> 17:26.736 align:middle
So it's, it's a strong move.

17:26.736 --> 17:28.156 align:middle
Sorry, your other question was?

17:28.306 --> 17:28.666 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:28.666 --> 17:30.806 align:middle
It was, is this about what
happened in the employment data

17:30.856 --> 17:32.966 align:middle
between this meeting
and the last meeting?

17:32.996 --> 17:34.536 align:middle
Or is this about the level

17:34.536 --> 17:36.826 align:middle
of the funds rate-the
high nominal level

17:36.826 --> 17:40.226 align:middle
of funds rate relative
to what might be expected

17:40.226 --> 17:42.166 align:middle
if you're trying to
maintain equilibrium?

17:42.166 --> 17:42.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:42.606 --> 17:48.096 align:middle
So I think it's about-we come
into this with a policy position

17:48.096 --> 17:50.266 align:middle
that was put in place,
as you know, I mentioned,

17:50.266 --> 17:54.276 align:middle
in July of 2023, which was
a time of high inflation

17:54.276 --> 17:55.356 align:middle
and very low unemployment.

17:55.916 --> 18:00.446 align:middle
We've been very patient about,
about reducing the policy rate.

18:00.446 --> 18:01.096 align:middle
We've waited.

18:01.096 --> 18:02.736 align:middle
Other central banks
around the world have cut,

18:02.856 --> 18:04.066 align:middle
many of them several times.

18:04.516 --> 18:05.996 align:middle
We've waited, and I think

18:05.996 --> 18:09.226 align:middle
that that patience has really
paid dividends in the form

18:09.226 --> 18:11.576 align:middle
of our confidence that
inflation is moving sustainably

18:11.576 --> 18:12.316 align:middle
under 2 percent.

18:13.196 --> 18:15.516 align:middle
So I think that is what
enables us to take this,

18:15.576 --> 18:16.626 align:middle
this strong move today.

18:17.016 --> 18:19.816 align:middle
I do not think that anyone
should look at this and say,

18:19.816 --> 18:21.126 align:middle
"Oh, this is the new pace."

18:21.706 --> 18:24.356 align:middle
You know, you have
to think about it

18:24.356 --> 18:25.486 align:middle
in terms of the base case.

18:25.486 --> 18:27.496 align:middle
Of course, what happens
will happen.

18:27.496 --> 18:31.346 align:middle
So, so, in the base case, what
you see is-look at the SEP,

18:31.346 --> 18:33.576 align:middle
you see cuts moving along.

18:33.576 --> 18:35.926 align:middle
The sense of this is
we're recalibrating policy

18:36.276 --> 18:39.486 align:middle
down over time to a
more neutral level.

18:40.136 --> 18:42.316 align:middle
And we're moving at the pace

18:42.446 --> 18:44.786 align:middle
that we think is
appropriate given developments

18:44.786 --> 18:46.246 align:middle
in the economy and
the base case.

18:47.246 --> 18:49.826 align:middle
The economy can develop in a way
that would cause us to go faster

18:49.826 --> 18:52.436 align:middle
or slower, but that's
what the base case is.

18:52.856 --> 18:53.256 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:53.256 --> 18:55.596 align:middle
And, if I could follow up on
the balance sheet, in 2019,

18:55.596 --> 18:57.076 align:middle
when you did the
mid-cycle adjustment,

18:57.146 --> 18:58.956 align:middle
you ceased the balance
sheet runoff.

18:59.546 --> 19:01.086 align:middle
With a larger cut today,

19:01.496 --> 19:03.796 align:middle
is there any-should there
be any signal inferred

19:04.236 --> 19:07.936 align:middle
about how the Committee
would approach "end state"

19:07.936 --> 19:10.366 align:middle
on the balance sheet policy?

19:10.586 --> 19:11.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:11.356 --> 19:13.596 align:middle
So, in the current situation,

19:13.596 --> 19:15.446 align:middle
reserves have really
been stable.

19:15.446 --> 19:16.216 align:middle
They haven't come down.

19:16.216 --> 19:18.796 align:middle
So reserves are still
abundant and expected

19:18.796 --> 19:20.546 align:middle
to remain so for some time.

19:20.546 --> 19:22.436 align:middle
As you know, the shrinkage

19:22.436 --> 19:23.496 align:middle
in our balance sheet
has really come

19:23.496 --> 19:25.126 align:middle
out of the overnight
RRP [facility].

19:25.596 --> 19:28.656 align:middle
So I think what that tells you
is, we're not thinking about,

19:28.776 --> 19:32.176 align:middle
about stopping runoff
because of this at all.

19:32.236 --> 19:35.716 align:middle
We know that these two things
can happen side by side.

19:35.716 --> 19:38.926 align:middle
In a sense, they're both a
form of normalization, and so,

19:38.926 --> 19:41.566 align:middle
for a time, you can have
the balance sheet shrinking

19:41.566 --> 19:42.726 align:middle
but also be cutting rates.

19:43.286 --> 19:43.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:44.276 --> 19:46.746 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

19:47.296 --> 19:47.696 align:middle
Thank you.

19:47.696 --> 19:49.276 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

19:49.276 --> 19:51.636 align:middle
Just following up on Jeanna's
question on rising unemployment.

19:51.986 --> 19:54.626 align:middle
Is it your view that
this is just a function

19:54.706 --> 19:57.476 align:middle
of a normalizing labor
market amid improved supply,

19:57.866 --> 19:59.166 align:middle
or is there anything to suggest

19:59.206 --> 20:02.096 align:middle
that something more concerning
perhaps is taking place here,

20:02.096 --> 20:05.356 align:middle
given that other metrics of
labor demand have softened too?

20:05.936 --> 20:08.656 align:middle
And I guess, in direct
follow-up to Jeanna,

20:08.656 --> 20:12.616 align:middle
do you not-why should we not
expect a further deterioration

20:12.616 --> 20:16.206 align:middle
in labor market conditions if
policy is still restrictive?

20:16.206 --> 20:16.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:16.666 --> 20:18.556 align:middle
So I think what we're seeing is,

20:18.556 --> 20:22.796 align:middle
clearly labor market conditions
have cooled off by any measure,

20:23.196 --> 20:27.056 align:middle
as I talked about in Jackson
Hole, and-but they're still

20:27.056 --> 20:29.596 align:middle
at a level-the level of those
conditions is actually pretty

20:29.596 --> 20:31.756 align:middle
close to what I would
call maximum employment.

20:32.236 --> 20:35.586 align:middle
So you're close to mandate,
maybe at mandate, on that.

20:36.236 --> 20:39.236 align:middle
So what's driving it?

20:39.236 --> 20:42.016 align:middle
Clearly, clearly payroll
job creation has moved

20:42.016 --> 20:45.206 align:middle
down over the last few months,
and this bears watching.

20:45.206 --> 20:49.716 align:middle
Many-By many other measures,
the labor market has returned to

20:49.716 --> 20:53.176 align:middle
or below 2019 levels,
which was a very good,

20:53.386 --> 20:57.226 align:middle
strong labor market, but this
is more sort of 2018, '17.

20:57.646 --> 21:00.946 align:middle
So the labor market bears
close-bears close watching,

21:00.946 --> 21:02.896 align:middle
and we'll be giving it that.

21:03.116 --> 21:05.586 align:middle
But ultimately, we
think-we believe,

21:06.066 --> 21:08.506 align:middle
with an appropriate
recalibration of our policy,

21:08.506 --> 21:11.626 align:middle
that we can continue to
see the economy growing,

21:12.046 --> 21:13.276 align:middle
and that will support
the labor market.

21:13.396 --> 21:15.666 align:middle
In the meantime, if
you look at the growth

21:15.666 --> 21:18.006 align:middle
in economic activity
data, retail sales data

21:18.006 --> 21:20.996 align:middle
that we just got,
second-quarter GDP-all

21:20.996 --> 21:22.276 align:middle
of this indicates an economy

21:22.276 --> 21:24.896 align:middle
that is still growing
at a solid pace.

21:25.286 --> 21:28.156 align:middle
So that should also support
the labor market over time.

21:28.736 --> 21:31.676 align:middle
So-but again, we're-it
bears watching.

21:31.676 --> 21:32.236 align:middle
And we're watching.

21:32.696 --> 21:33.086 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

21:33.236 --> 21:36.506 align:middle
And just on the point about
starting to see rising layoffs,

21:36.506 --> 21:40.246 align:middle
if that were to happen, wouldn't
the Committee already be too

21:40.246 --> 21:41.956 align:middle
late in terms of
avoiding a recession?

21:41.956 --> 21:42.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:42.726 --> 21:45.406 align:middle
So we're-that's-you know,
our plan, of course,

21:45.406 --> 21:48.146 align:middle
has been to begin to recalibrate
and we're-as you know,

21:48.146 --> 21:51.826 align:middle
we're not seeing rising claims,
we're not seeing rising layoffs,

21:51.826 --> 21:54.206 align:middle
we're not seeing that, and we're
not hearing that from companies

21:54.206 --> 21:55.866 align:middle
that that's something that's
getting ready to happen.

21:56.256 --> 21:58.206 align:middle
So we're not waiting for
that, because, you know,

21:58.206 --> 22:01.946 align:middle
there is-there is
thinking that the time

22:01.946 --> 22:05.806 align:middle
to support the labor market is
when-is when it's strong and not

22:05.806 --> 22:07.186 align:middle
when we begin to
see the layoffs.

22:07.186 --> 22:09.136 align:middle
There's some lore on that.

22:09.136 --> 22:10.406 align:middle
So that's the situation
we're in.

22:10.406 --> 22:13.156 align:middle
We have, in fact, begun
the cutting cycle now,

22:13.766 --> 22:15.966 align:middle
and we'll be watching-and
that'll be one

22:15.966 --> 22:16.986 align:middle
of the factors that we consider.

22:16.986 --> 22:19.066 align:middle
Of course, we're going to look
at the totality of the data

22:19.066 --> 22:20.826 align:middle
as we make these decisions
meeting by meeting.

22:22.206 --> 22:22.716 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:23.616 --> 22:24.866 align:middle
Michael McKee.

22:25.086 --> 22:25.356 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

22:25.566 --> 22:27.756 align:middle
Michael McKee with
Bloomberg TV and Radio.

22:27.756 --> 22:32.176 align:middle
To follow up on that, what
would constitute for you

22:32.176 --> 22:33.796 align:middle
and the Committee
a deterioration

22:33.796 --> 22:34.686 align:middle
in the labor market?

22:34.686 --> 22:38.206 align:middle
You're pricing in, basically
by the end of next year,

22:38.206 --> 22:40.546 align:middle
200 basis points of cuts just

22:40.546 --> 22:43.216 align:middle
to maintain a higher
unemployment rate.

22:43.816 --> 22:48.786 align:middle
Would you be moving to a more
preemptive monetary policy

22:48.786 --> 22:51.606 align:middle
style-rather than, as
you did with inflation,

22:51.996 --> 22:54.306 align:middle
waiting until the data
gave you a signal?

22:54.516 --> 22:55.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:55.986 --> 22:59.006 align:middle
We're going to be watching
all of the data, right?

22:59.096 --> 23:01.236 align:middle
So if-as I mentioned
in my remarks,

23:01.236 --> 23:04.786 align:middle
if the labor market were
to slow unexpectedly,

23:05.326 --> 23:06.906 align:middle
then we have the
ability to react

23:06.906 --> 23:08.726 align:middle
to that by, by cutting faster.

23:08.726 --> 23:11.766 align:middle
We're also going to be looking
at our other mandate, though.

23:12.026 --> 23:15.246 align:middle
We are more-we have
greater confidence now

23:15.246 --> 23:17.086 align:middle
that inflation is moving
down to 2 percent, but,

23:17.186 --> 23:20.166 align:middle
at the same time, our
plan is that we will be

23:20.166 --> 23:24.216 align:middle
at 2 percent, you
know, over time.

23:24.216 --> 23:26.676 align:middle
So-and policy, we think,
is still restrictive,

23:26.676 --> 23:28.196 align:middle
so that should still
be happening.

23:28.866 --> 23:29.296 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

23:29.296 --> 23:31.736 align:middle
I'm just curious as to
how sensitive you'll be

23:32.086 --> 23:34.776 align:middle
to the labor market, since
you forecast we are going

23:34.776 --> 23:37.146 align:middle
to see higher unemployment
and it is going

23:37.146 --> 23:38.566 align:middle
to take a significant amount

23:38.566 --> 23:41.396 align:middle
of monetary easing
to just maintain it.

23:41.766 --> 23:42.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:43.436 --> 23:45.886 align:middle
So, you know, what I would
say is, we don't think we need

23:45.886 --> 23:48.496 align:middle
to see further loosening
in labor market conditions

23:48.496 --> 23:50.086 align:middle
to get inflation
down to 2 percent.

23:50.776 --> 23:52.936 align:middle
But we have a dual mandate,

23:53.096 --> 23:58.776 align:middle
and I think you can take this
whole action as-take a step

23:58.776 --> 24:00.226 align:middle
back, what have we
been trying to achieve?

24:00.226 --> 24:01.826 align:middle
We're trying to achieve
a situation

24:01.826 --> 24:04.496 align:middle
where we restore price
stability without the kind

24:04.496 --> 24:07.746 align:middle
of painful increase
in unemployment

24:08.196 --> 24:10.746 align:middle
that has come sometimes
with disinflation.

24:10.976 --> 24:12.056 align:middle
That's what we're trying to do.

24:12.056 --> 24:16.386 align:middle
And I think you can take
today's action as a sign

24:16.386 --> 24:18.516 align:middle
of our strong commitment
to achieve that goal.

24:18.516 --> 24:18.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:20.656 --> 24:23.236 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

24:24.366 --> 24:25.086 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

24:25.176 --> 24:26.376 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

24:26.486 --> 24:27.656 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

24:28.236 --> 24:31.256 align:middle
You're describing this view that
you don't think you're behind

24:31.256 --> 24:32.256 align:middle
when it comes to the job market.

24:32.256 --> 24:34.806 align:middle
Can you walk us through
the specific data points

24:34.806 --> 24:36.586 align:middle
that you found to
be most helpful

24:36.586 --> 24:37.976 align:middle
in the discussions
at this meeting?

24:37.976 --> 24:40.256 align:middle
You've mentioned a couple, but
would you be able to walk us

24:40.256 --> 24:42.446 align:middle
through what that dashboard told
you, as far as what you know

24:42.446 --> 24:43.366 align:middle
about the job market now?

24:43.756 --> 24:44.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:44.646 --> 24:46.346 align:middle
Sure. So we'll start
with unemployment,

24:46.346 --> 24:48.896 align:middle
which is the single most
important one probably.

24:48.896 --> 24:50.206 align:middle
You're at 4.2 percent.

24:51.056 --> 24:53.656 align:middle
That's, you know-I know it's
higher than we were used

24:53.656 --> 24:57.816 align:middle
to seeing, numbers in the mid-
and even below mid-3s last year,

24:57.816 --> 24:59.936 align:middle
but, if you look
back over the sweep

24:59.936 --> 25:01.556 align:middle
of the years, that's,
that's a low.

25:01.556 --> 25:03.356 align:middle
That's a very healthy
unemployment rate.

25:03.456 --> 25:05.886 align:middle
And anything in the low 4s is,

25:05.976 --> 25:08.076 align:middle
is a really-is a
good labor market.

25:08.106 --> 25:09.586 align:middle
So that's one thing.

25:09.586 --> 25:11.496 align:middle
Participation is at high levels.

25:11.636 --> 25:15.606 align:middle
It's, you know-we've had-we're
right-adjusted for demographics

25:15.606 --> 25:19.176 align:middle
for aging, participation's
at pretty high levels.

25:19.176 --> 25:19.766 align:middle
That's a good thing.

25:20.136 --> 25:24.746 align:middle
Wages are still a bit above what
would be their-wage increases,

25:24.746 --> 25:27.776 align:middle
rather, are still just a bit
above where they would be

25:27.776 --> 25:29.936 align:middle
over the very longer
term to be consistent

25:29.936 --> 25:32.276 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation,
but they're very much coming

25:32.276 --> 25:33.986 align:middle
down to what that
sustainable level is.

25:33.986 --> 25:35.776 align:middle
So we feel good about that.

25:35.776 --> 25:39.456 align:middle
Vacancies over-per
unemployed is back

25:39.456 --> 25:41.236 align:middle
to what is still a
very strong level.

25:41.236 --> 25:42.476 align:middle
It's not as high as it was.

25:42.766 --> 25:44.706 align:middle
That number reached two
to one, two vacancies

25:44.706 --> 25:46.836 align:middle
for every unemployed person.

25:46.836 --> 25:50.246 align:middle
As measured, it's now
below-it's around one.

25:50.246 --> 25:52.586 align:middle
But that's still-that's still a
very good number, I would say.

25:53.126 --> 25:57.186 align:middle
You know, quits have come
back down to normal levels.

25:57.336 --> 25:58.376 align:middle
I mean, I could go on and on.

25:58.376 --> 26:00.366 align:middle
There are many, many
employment indicators.

26:00.366 --> 26:00.946 align:middle
What do they say?

26:01.266 --> 26:03.446 align:middle
They say this is still
a solid labor market.

26:03.886 --> 26:05.616 align:middle
The question isn't the
level; the question is

26:05.616 --> 26:08.186 align:middle
that there has been
change, particularly

26:08.186 --> 26:11.806 align:middle
over the last few months, and,
you know, so what we say is,

26:11.926 --> 26:14.736 align:middle
as the risks, the upside risks,

26:14.786 --> 26:18.376 align:middle
to inflation have really
come down, the downside risks

26:18.376 --> 26:19.656 align:middle
to employment have increased.

26:19.856 --> 26:24.816 align:middle
And, and because we have been
patient and held our fire

26:24.816 --> 26:27.236 align:middle
on cutting while, while
inflation has come down,

26:27.236 --> 26:29.506 align:middle
I think we're now in
a very good position

26:29.996 --> 26:32.476 align:middle
to manage the risks
to both of our goals.

26:33.206 --> 26:33.516 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

26:33.516 --> 26:35.916 align:middle
And what do you expect to
learn between now and November

26:35.916 --> 26:38.376 align:middle
that will help inform the scale
of the cut at the next meeting?

26:39.356 --> 26:39.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:39.636 --> 26:41.076 align:middle
You know, more data, the usual.

26:41.936 --> 26:43.756 align:middle
Don't look for anything else.

26:44.006 --> 26:45.456 align:middle
We'll see another labor report.

26:45.456 --> 26:49.536 align:middle
We'll see another jobs report.

26:49.536 --> 26:51.016 align:middle
I think we get a-we might,

26:51.016 --> 26:53.296 align:middle
actually-we get two-we get a
second jobs report on the day

26:53.296 --> 26:55.216 align:middle
of the meeting, I
think, or no, no,

26:55.296 --> 26:57.626 align:middle
on the Friday before
the meeting.

26:57.766 --> 27:00.106 align:middle
So-and inflation data.

27:00.106 --> 27:01.196 align:middle
We'll get all this data.

27:01.196 --> 27:01.856 align:middle
We'll be watching.

27:01.856 --> 27:06.486 align:middle
You know, it's always a question
of, look at the incoming data

27:06.796 --> 27:09.436 align:middle
and ask what are the
implications of that data

27:09.436 --> 27:13.906 align:middle
for the evolving outlook and
the balance of risks and then go

27:13.906 --> 27:16.086 align:middle
through our process and think,
what's the right thing to do?

27:16.496 --> 27:20.486 align:middle
Is policy where we want it to
be to foster the achievement

27:20.486 --> 27:21.546 align:middle
of our goals over time?

27:21.546 --> 27:24.676 align:middle
So that's what it is, and
that's what we'll be doing.

27:26.216 --> 27:26.516 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:27.236 --> 27:28.016 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

27:28.406 --> 27:29.826 align:middle
Thanks. Hi, Chair Powell.

27:29.886 --> 27:30.966 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

27:31.346 --> 27:34.206 align:middle
We've only been running a little
above 100,000 jobs a month

27:34.206 --> 27:35.646 align:middle
on payroll the last
three months.

27:35.646 --> 27:38.496 align:middle
Do you view that level of
job creation as worrying

27:38.496 --> 27:40.926 align:middle
or alarming, or would you
be-would you be content

27:40.926 --> 27:42.526 align:middle
if we were to kind of
stick at that level?

27:43.006 --> 27:45.266 align:middle
And relatedly, you know,
one of the welcome trends

27:45.266 --> 27:47.946 align:middle
of the last couple of years has
been labor markets seen coming

27:47.946 --> 27:50.776 align:middle
out through job openings
falling, rather than job losses.

27:51.136 --> 27:53.126 align:middle
Do you think that trend
has further to run,

27:53.126 --> 27:55.956 align:middle
or do you see risk that further
labor market cooling will have

27:55.956 --> 27:56.876 align:middle
to come through job losses?

27:57.416 --> 27:57.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:58.636 --> 28:01.266 align:middle
So, on the job creation,
it depends on-it depends

28:01.266 --> 28:03.376 align:middle
on the inflows, right?

28:03.376 --> 28:05.456 align:middle
So, if you're having
millions of people come

28:05.456 --> 28:08.686 align:middle
into the labor force then, and
you're creating 100,000 jobs,

28:09.146 --> 28:10.386 align:middle
you're going to see
unemployment go up.

28:10.706 --> 28:14.466 align:middle
So it really depends on what's
the trend underlying the

28:14.466 --> 28:16.836 align:middle
volatility of people
coming into the country.

28:16.836 --> 28:20.776 align:middle
We understand there's been quite
an influx across the borders,

28:21.346 --> 28:24.136 align:middle
and that has actually been one
of the things that's, that's,

28:24.206 --> 28:26.746 align:middle
that's allowed the
unemployment rate to rise.

28:26.746 --> 28:29.096 align:middle
And the other thing is just
the slower hiring rate,

28:29.146 --> 28:31.336 align:middle
which is something we
also watch carefully.

28:31.336 --> 28:34.986 align:middle
So it does depend on what's
happening on the supply side.

28:35.586 --> 28:40.176 align:middle
On the Beveridge curve
question, yes, so we all felt,

28:40.336 --> 28:42.966 align:middle
on the Committee, not
all, but I think everyone

28:42.966 --> 28:46.456 align:middle
on the Committee felt that
job openings were so elevated

28:46.456 --> 28:48.926 align:middle
that they could fall a long
way before you hit the part

28:48.926 --> 28:51.446 align:middle
of the curve where
job openings turned

28:51.446 --> 28:54.296 align:middle
into higher unemployment,
job loss.

28:54.866 --> 28:58.756 align:middle
And yes, I mean, I
think we are-it's hard

28:58.756 --> 28:59.936 align:middle
to know that-you can't
know these things

28:59.936 --> 29:02.276 align:middle
with great precision,
but certainly it appears

29:02.276 --> 29:04.446 align:middle
that we're very close to
that point, if not at it,

29:04.796 --> 29:06.066 align:middle
so that further declines

29:06.066 --> 29:11.756 align:middle
in job openings will translate
more directly into unemployment.

29:12.236 --> 29:15.466 align:middle
But it's been-it's
been a great ride down.

29:15.466 --> 29:19.576 align:middle
I mean, we've seen a lot
of, of tightness come

29:19.576 --> 29:20.866 align:middle
out of the labor
market in that form

29:20.866 --> 29:24.786 align:middle
without it resulting
in lower employment.

29:25.826 --> 29:27.236 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:27.476 --> 29:28.376 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

29:29.266 --> 29:29.946 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

29:30.036 --> 29:31.576 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

29:31.926 --> 29:35.056 align:middle
So we've heard some speculation
that you may be going

29:35.056 --> 29:37.566 align:middle
with the federal funds rate
to 3½, maybe under 4 percent.

29:37.956 --> 29:39.656 align:middle
There's basically
an entire generation

29:39.656 --> 29:43.566 align:middle
that has experienced zero or
near-zero federal funds rate,

29:43.776 --> 29:45.586 align:middle
and some think we're heading
in that direction again.

29:45.586 --> 29:47.726 align:middle
What's the likelihood that
cheap money is now the norm?

29:47.726 --> 29:47.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:48.906 --> 29:50.286 align:middle
So this is a question-and
you mean

29:50.466 --> 29:52.526 align:middle
after we get through
all of this?

29:52.526 --> 29:54.966 align:middle
It's just-great question
that we just,

29:55.356 --> 29:56.596 align:middle
we can only speculate about.

29:57.086 --> 30:00.356 align:middle
Intuitively, most-many,
many people anyway,

30:00.356 --> 30:02.936 align:middle
would say we're probably
not going back to that era

30:02.936 --> 30:04.596 align:middle
where there were
trillions of dollars

30:04.656 --> 30:06.776 align:middle
of sovereign bonds trading

30:06.776 --> 30:09.586 align:middle
at negative rates-long-term
bonds trading at negative rates.

30:10.276 --> 30:13.516 align:middle
And it looked like the neutral
rate was-might even be negative,

30:13.516 --> 30:16.546 align:middle
so-and it was-people were
issuing debt at negative rates.

30:16.996 --> 30:19.966 align:middle
It seems that's so far away now.

30:20.616 --> 30:23.436 align:middle
My own sense is that we're
not going back to that.

30:24.026 --> 30:26.026 align:middle
But, you know, honestly,
we're going to find out.

30:26.196 --> 30:29.896 align:middle
But, you know, it
feels-it feels to me

30:30.076 --> 30:34.426 align:middle
that the neutral rate is
probably significantly higher

30:34.426 --> 30:35.576 align:middle
than it was back then.

30:36.146 --> 30:36.956 align:middle
How high is it?

30:37.156 --> 30:38.746 align:middle
I don't-I just don't
think we know.

30:38.746 --> 30:40.416 align:middle
It's-again, we only
know it by its works.

30:40.786 --> 30:41.246 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

30:41.246 --> 30:43.176 align:middle
And one more, how do you
respond to the criticism

30:43.176 --> 30:45.726 align:middle
that will likely come that
a deeper rate cut now,

30:45.726 --> 30:47.816 align:middle
before the election, has
some political motivations?

30:48.416 --> 30:48.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:48.996 --> 30:51.816 align:middle
Yeah, so, you know, this is my
fourth presidential election

30:52.076 --> 30:55.886 align:middle
at the Fed, and, you know,
it's always the same.

30:55.886 --> 30:58.766 align:middle
We're always-we're always
going into this meeting,

30:58.766 --> 31:02.186 align:middle
in particular, and asking,
what's the right thing to do

31:02.346 --> 31:03.276 align:middle
for the people we serve?

31:03.416 --> 31:07.396 align:middle
And we do that, and we
make a decision as a group,

31:08.136 --> 31:09.296 align:middle
and then we announce it.

31:09.416 --> 31:10.976 align:middle
And it's-that's always
what it is.

31:10.976 --> 31:12.476 align:middle
It's never about anything else.

31:12.476 --> 31:16.586 align:middle
Nothing else is discussed,
and I would also point

31:16.586 --> 31:19.196 align:middle
out that the things that we
do really affect economic

31:19.196 --> 31:22.086 align:middle
conditions for the
most part with a lag.

31:22.686 --> 31:25.296 align:middle
So, nonetheless,
this is what we do.

31:25.296 --> 31:28.176 align:middle
Our job is to support
the economy on behalf

31:28.176 --> 31:29.036 align:middle
of the American people.

31:29.436 --> 31:30.866 align:middle
And, if we get it right,

31:31.216 --> 31:33.496 align:middle
this will benefit the
American people significantly.

31:33.496 --> 31:37.266 align:middle
So this really concentrates
the mind, and, you know,

31:37.646 --> 31:40.016 align:middle
it's something we all
take very, very seriously.

31:40.256 --> 31:41.916 align:middle
We don't put up any
other filters.

31:41.916 --> 31:44.906 align:middle
I think if you start doing that,
I don't know where you stop.

31:45.116 --> 31:46.146 align:middle
And so we just don't do that.

31:47.826 --> 31:48.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:48.156 --> 31:49.206 align:middle
Jo Ling. JO LING KENT.

31:51.416 --> 31:52.936 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

31:52.936 --> 31:55.356 align:middle
I'm Jo Ling Kent with CBS News.

31:55.356 --> 31:57.696 align:middle
My first question
is, very simply,

31:57.776 --> 32:01.286 align:middle
what message are you trying
to send American consumers,

32:01.286 --> 32:04.556 align:middle
the American people, with
this unusually large rate cut?

32:05.376 --> 32:06.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:06.546 --> 32:09.216 align:middle
I would just say that, you
know, the U.S. economy is

32:09.216 --> 32:13.266 align:middle
in a good place, and, and our
decision today is designed

32:13.266 --> 32:13.746 align:middle
to keep it there.

32:14.486 --> 32:17.096 align:middle
More specifically, the economy
is growing at a solid pace,

32:17.196 --> 32:20.546 align:middle
inflation is coming down closer
to our 2 percent objective

32:20.546 --> 32:24.306 align:middle
over time, and the labor
market is still in solid shape.

32:24.616 --> 32:28.546 align:middle
So our intention is really
to maintain the strength

32:28.546 --> 32:30.356 align:middle
that we currently see
in the U.S. economy.

32:30.876 --> 32:33.886 align:middle
And we'll do that by returning
rates from their high level,

32:33.886 --> 32:36.736 align:middle
which has really been-the
purpose of which has been

32:36.736 --> 32:38.036 align:middle
to get inflation under control.

32:39.016 --> 32:41.186 align:middle
We're, we're going to
move those down over time

32:41.186 --> 32:43.316 align:middle
to a more normal
level over time.

32:43.716 --> 32:44.476 align:middle
JO LING KENT.

32:44.476 --> 32:47.286 align:middle
Just have a follow-up to that.

32:47.286 --> 32:50.216 align:middle
Listening to you talk about
inflation moving meaningfully

32:50.216 --> 32:51.046 align:middle
down to 2 percent,

32:51.926 --> 32:55.196 align:middle
is the Federal Reserve
effectively declaring a decisive

32:55.196 --> 32:57.526 align:middle
victory over inflation
and rising prices?

32:57.526 --> 32:58.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:59.256 --> 33:00.116 align:middle
No, we're not.

33:00.426 --> 33:05.536 align:middle
So inflation, you know-what we
say is we want inflation-the

33:05.536 --> 33:08.346 align:middle
goal is to have inflation
move down to 2 percent

33:08.786 --> 33:09.986 align:middle
on a sustainable basis.

33:10.636 --> 33:13.456 align:middle
And, you know, we're
not really-we're close,

33:13.626 --> 33:14.996 align:middle
but we're not really
at 2 percent.

33:15.296 --> 33:18.396 align:middle
And I think we're going to
want to see it be, you know,

33:18.396 --> 33:22.136 align:middle
around 2 percent and close
to 2 percent for some time,

33:22.216 --> 33:25.686 align:middle
but we're certainly not
doing-we're not saying

33:25.856 --> 33:27.316 align:middle
"mission accomplished"
or anything like that.

33:27.356 --> 33:29.396 align:middle
But I have to say,
though, we're encouraged

33:29.396 --> 33:30.786 align:middle
by the progress that
we have made.

33:32.426 --> 33:33.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:33.756 --> 33:36.666 align:middle
Catarina. CATARINA SARAIVA.

33:36.986 --> 33:39.216 align:middle
Hi. Catarina Saraiva
with Bloomberg News.

33:39.686 --> 33:43.896 align:middle
I just would love to know kind
of how the Committee is thinking

33:43.896 --> 33:47.316 align:middle
about the persistence we've
seen in housing inflation.

33:48.296 --> 33:51.876 align:middle
You know, do you think you
can return to 2 percent

33:52.286 --> 33:54.936 align:middle
with housing inflation
where it is?

33:55.246 --> 33:57.116 align:middle
Yeah. CHAIR POWELL.

33:57.116 --> 34:01.806 align:middle
So housing inflation is the-is
the one piece that is kind

34:01.896 --> 34:03.836 align:middle
of dragging a bit, if I can say.

34:04.376 --> 34:08.726 align:middle
We know that market rents are
doing what we would want them

34:08.726 --> 34:11.586 align:middle
to do, which is to be moving
up at relatively low levels,

34:11.886 --> 34:14.806 align:middle
but they're not rolling
over-the leases that are rolling

34:14.806 --> 34:20.066 align:middle
over are not coming down as
much and OER is coming in high.

34:20.066 --> 34:23.926 align:middle
So, you know, it's been
slower than we expected.

34:23.926 --> 34:26.926 align:middle
I think we now understand that
it's going to take some time

34:26.926 --> 34:28.996 align:middle
for those lower market
rents to get into this.

34:28.996 --> 34:31.476 align:middle
But, you know, the
direction of travel is clear,

34:31.666 --> 34:35.056 align:middle
and as long as market
rents remain, you know,

34:35.646 --> 34:38.226 align:middle
relatively low inflation,
over time that will show up,

34:38.226 --> 34:41.626 align:middle
just the time it's taking
now, several years rather

34:41.626 --> 34:44.776 align:middle
than just one or two cycles
of annual lease renewals.

34:44.776 --> 34:47.746 align:middle
So that's-I think we
understand that now,

34:47.746 --> 34:50.106 align:middle
but I don't think the
outcome is in doubt again.

34:50.106 --> 34:53.266 align:middle
As long as market rents
remain under control,

34:53.686 --> 34:55.036 align:middle
the outcome is not as in doubt.

34:55.036 --> 35:00.886 align:middle
So I would say it's-the rest of
the portfolio-or of the elements

35:00.886 --> 35:03.886 align:middle
that go into core PCE inflation
have behaved pretty well.

35:04.446 --> 35:06.576 align:middle
You know, they're all-they
all have some volatility.

35:06.936 --> 35:09.186 align:middle
We will get down to 2
percent inflation, I believe,

35:09.526 --> 35:13.586 align:middle
and I believe that, ultimately,
we'll get what we need to get

35:13.586 --> 35:15.506 align:middle
out of the housing
services piece, too.

35:15.506 --> 35:16.206 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

35:16.686 --> 35:19.436 align:middle
Some of your colleagues
have expressed concern that,

35:19.436 --> 35:22.596 align:middle
with starting to cut rates,
you could re-ignite demand

35:22.706 --> 35:25.026 align:middle
in housing and see
prices go up even more.

35:25.526 --> 35:30.686 align:middle
What's the likelihood of that,
and how would you react to that?

35:31.586 --> 35:31.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:31.906 --> 35:35.706 align:middle
The housing market, it's hard
to-it's a game that-the housing

35:35.706 --> 35:39.376 align:middle
market is in part frozen because
of lock-in with low rates.

35:39.376 --> 35:41.226 align:middle
People don't want
to sell their homes.

35:41.226 --> 35:42.866 align:middle
So, because they have
a very low mortgage,

35:42.866 --> 35:44.536 align:middle
it would be quite
expensive to refinance.

35:44.536 --> 35:48.396 align:middle
As rates come down, people
will start to move more,

35:48.396 --> 35:50.176 align:middle
and that's probably
beginning to happen already.

35:50.576 --> 35:53.956 align:middle
But remember, when that
happens, you've got a seller,

35:53.956 --> 35:56.146 align:middle
but you've also got a
new buyer in many cases.

35:56.146 --> 36:02.406 align:middle
So it's not, you know, obvious
how much additional demand

36:02.406 --> 36:03.256 align:middle
that would make.

36:03.556 --> 36:07.536 align:middle
I mean, the real issue with
housing is that we have had,

36:07.536 --> 36:11.236 align:middle
and are on track to continue
to have, not enough housing.

36:11.766 --> 36:14.006 align:middle
And so it's going
to be challenging.

36:14.006 --> 36:17.396 align:middle
It's hard to find-to zone
lots that are in places

36:17.396 --> 36:18.276 align:middle
where people want to live.

36:18.896 --> 36:21.556 align:middle
It's-all of the aspects
of housing are more

36:22.286 --> 36:24.876 align:middle
and more difficult,
and, you know,

36:24.876 --> 36:26.436 align:middle
where are we going
to get the supply?

36:26.436 --> 36:29.316 align:middle
And this is not something that
the Fed can, can really fix.

36:29.666 --> 36:31.466 align:middle
But I think, as we
normalize rates,

36:31.466 --> 36:33.256 align:middle
you'll see the housing
market normalize.

36:33.946 --> 36:37.026 align:middle
And, I mean, ultimately by
getting inflation broadly down

36:37.026 --> 36:39.096 align:middle
and getting those
rates normalized

36:39.096 --> 36:42.046 align:middle
and getting the housing
cycle normalized,

36:42.046 --> 36:43.986 align:middle
that's the best thing we
can do for house holders.

36:43.986 --> 36:46.286 align:middle
And then the supply question
will have to be dealt

36:46.286 --> 36:48.156 align:middle
with by the market and
also by government.

36:49.566 --> 36:50.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:50.126 --> 36:53.096 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

36:53.156 --> 36:54.606 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

36:54.986 --> 36:58.526 align:middle
Just following up on some of
the labor market talk earlier,

36:58.776 --> 37:01.686 align:middle
you know, monetary
policy operates with long

37:01.686 --> 37:05.266 align:middle
and variable lags, and I'm
wondering how much you see being

37:05.266 --> 37:10.526 align:middle
able to keep the unemployment
rate from raising too much comes

37:10.526 --> 37:12.996 align:middle
from the fact that
you're starting to act now

37:12.996 --> 37:14.766 align:middle
and that's going to give
people more room to run

37:14.766 --> 37:17.376 align:middle
versus just the labor
market is strong.

37:18.026 --> 37:20.836 align:middle
And then, also, if I could,
following up on Nick's question,

37:21.346 --> 37:24.896 align:middle
do you see today's
50 basis point move

37:25.076 --> 37:28.606 align:middle
as partially a response to the
fact that you didn't cut in July

37:28.606 --> 37:30.166 align:middle
and that sort of gets
you to the same place?

37:30.696 --> 37:30.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:30.976 --> 37:33.376 align:middle
So you're right about lags,
but I would just point

37:33.376 --> 37:34.436 align:middle
to the overall economy.

37:34.436 --> 37:37.626 align:middle
You have an economy that
is growing at a solid pace.

37:38.056 --> 37:40.496 align:middle
If you look at forecasters
or talk to companies,

37:40.986 --> 37:44.476 align:middle
they'll say that they think
2025 should be a good year, too.

37:44.966 --> 37:49.406 align:middle
So there's no sense-the U.S.
economy is basically fine

37:49.876 --> 37:52.736 align:middle
if you talk to market
participants, I mean, you know,

37:52.786 --> 37:55.726 align:middle
business people who are actually
out there doing business.

37:56.106 --> 37:59.636 align:middle
So I think, you know, I
think our move is timely.

38:00.206 --> 38:02.266 align:middle
I do. And, as I said,
you can-you can see our,

38:02.826 --> 38:07.536 align:middle
our 50 basis point move as
a commitment to make sure

38:07.536 --> 38:08.466 align:middle
that we don't fall behind.

38:09.326 --> 38:11.486 align:middle
So you're really asking
about-your second question,

38:11.486 --> 38:12.596 align:middle
you're asking about July.

38:12.596 --> 38:19.476 align:middle
And I guess if you ask if we'd
gotten the July report before

38:19.876 --> 38:21.736 align:middle
the meeting, would we have cut?

38:21.846 --> 38:23.596 align:middle
We might well have.

38:23.596 --> 38:26.386 align:middle
We didn't make that decision,
but we might well have.

38:26.386 --> 38:28.186 align:middle
I think that's not-you know,

38:29.186 --> 38:33.576 align:middle
that doesn't really answer the
question that we ask ourselves,

38:33.576 --> 38:34.496 align:middle
which is, let's look,

38:34.606 --> 38:36.526 align:middle
you know-at this meeting
we're looking back

38:36.596 --> 38:40.226 align:middle
to the July employment report,
the August employment report,

38:40.226 --> 38:43.416 align:middle
the two CPI reports, one
of which came, of course,

38:43.416 --> 38:46.386 align:middle
during blackout, and all of the
other things that I mentioned.

38:46.386 --> 38:47.496 align:middle
We're looking at
all of those things,

38:47.496 --> 38:48.426 align:middle
and we're asking ourselves,

38:48.676 --> 38:51.446 align:middle
what's the right-what's
the policy stance we need

38:51.446 --> 38:51.856 align:middle
to move to?

38:51.856 --> 38:55.516 align:middle
We knew-it's clear that
we-clearly, literally,

38:55.516 --> 38:58.776 align:middle
everyone on the Committee
agreed that it's time to move.

38:59.616 --> 39:02.696 align:middle
It's just how big-how fast do
you go, and what do you think

39:02.696 --> 39:03.606 align:middle
about the paths forward.

39:03.606 --> 39:06.916 align:middle
So this decision we made
today had broad support

39:06.916 --> 39:08.846 align:middle
on the Committee, and I've
discussed the path ahead.

39:10.856 --> 39:11.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:11.166 --> 39:11.426 align:middle
Elizabeth.

39:12.896 --> 39:13.516 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

39:13.516 --> 39:14.136 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

39:14.136 --> 39:15.806 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

39:16.206 --> 39:17.966 align:middle
Mortgage rates have
already been dropping

39:17.966 --> 39:19.596 align:middle
in anticipation of
this announcement.

39:19.596 --> 39:22.916 align:middle
How much more should
borrowers expect those rates

39:22.916 --> 39:24.676 align:middle
to drop over the next year?

39:25.006 --> 39:26.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:26.186 --> 39:27.076 align:middle
Very hard for me to say.

39:27.186 --> 39:28.566 align:middle
That's-from our standpoint,

39:28.906 --> 39:30.926 align:middle
I can't really speak
to mortgage rates.

39:30.926 --> 39:35.696 align:middle
I will say that'll depend
on how the economy evolves.

39:36.306 --> 39:38.986 align:middle
Our intention, though,

39:38.986 --> 39:42.876 align:middle
is-we think that our policy
was appropriately restrictive.

39:43.656 --> 39:49.706 align:middle
We think that it's time to begin
the process of recalibrating it

39:50.336 --> 39:52.786 align:middle
to a level that's more neutral,
rather than restrictive.

39:52.786 --> 39:57.026 align:middle
We expect that process to
take some time, as you can see

39:57.026 --> 39:58.876 align:middle
in the projections
that we released today.

39:59.096 --> 40:02.606 align:middle
And as-if things work out
according to that forecast,

40:03.486 --> 40:05.416 align:middle
other rates in the economy
will come down as well.

40:05.746 --> 40:09.716 align:middle
However, the rate at which those
things happen will really depend

40:09.716 --> 40:10.916 align:middle
on how the economy performs.

40:10.916 --> 40:12.916 align:middle
We can't see-we can't
look a year ahead

40:12.916 --> 40:14.826 align:middle
and know what the economy is
to be doing-going to be doing.

40:15.396 --> 40:15.866 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

40:15.996 --> 40:18.536 align:middle
What's your message to
households who are frustrated

40:18.926 --> 40:21.466 align:middle
that home prices have
still stayed so high

40:21.466 --> 40:22.506 align:middle
as rates have been high?

40:23.356 --> 40:25.166 align:middle
What do you say to
those households?

40:25.166 --> 40:25.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:25.586 --> 40:28.356 align:middle
Well, what I can
say to the public is

40:28.536 --> 40:33.436 align:middle
that we had the highest-we
had a burst of inflation.

40:34.336 --> 40:38.486 align:middle
Many other countries around
the world had a similar burst

40:38.486 --> 40:42.046 align:middle
of inflation, and, when that
happens, part of the answer is

40:42.046 --> 40:44.956 align:middle
that we raise interest rates in
order to cool the economy off

40:44.956 --> 40:47.346 align:middle
in order to reduce
inflationary pressures.

40:48.186 --> 40:53.036 align:middle
It's not something that people
experience as pleasant, but,

40:54.276 --> 40:58.586 align:middle
at the end, what you get
is low inflation restored,

40:58.676 --> 40:59.876 align:middle
price stability restored.

41:00.466 --> 41:03.176 align:middle
And a good definition of
price stability is that people

41:03.176 --> 41:05.306 align:middle
in their daily decisions,
they're not thinking

41:05.306 --> 41:06.346 align:middle
about inflation anymore.

41:06.896 --> 41:08.526 align:middle
That's where everyone
wants to be,

41:08.526 --> 41:11.526 align:middle
is back to what's
inflation, you know?

41:11.556 --> 41:13.156 align:middle
Just keep it low,
keep it stable.

41:13.606 --> 41:14.466 align:middle
We're restoring that.

41:14.466 --> 41:20.826 align:middle
So what we're going through now
really restores-it will benefit

41:20.826 --> 41:23.086 align:middle
people over a long
period of time.

41:23.606 --> 41:25.816 align:middle
Price stability benefits
everybody over a long period

41:25.816 --> 41:27.646 align:middle
of time, just by virtue of
the fact that they don't have

41:27.686 --> 41:28.486 align:middle
to deal with inflation.

41:28.756 --> 41:29.906 align:middle
So that's what's been going on,

41:30.436 --> 41:32.066 align:middle
and I think we've
made real progress.

41:32.066 --> 41:34.556 align:middle
I completely-we don't
tell people how to think

41:34.556 --> 41:35.836 align:middle
about the economy, of course.

41:35.896 --> 41:37.896 align:middle
And, of course, people are
[still] experiencing high

41:37.946 --> 41:40.056 align:middle
prices, as opposed
to high inflation.

41:40.396 --> 41:42.016 align:middle
And we understand
that's painful.

41:44.246 --> 41:45.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:45.246 --> 41:50.586 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

41:52.416 --> 41:53.046 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

41:53.046 --> 41:54.706 align:middle
Greg Robb from Marketwatch.com.

41:56.296 --> 41:58.596 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
go through-you said just

41:58.756 --> 42:01.776 align:middle
at the beginning that,
coming into the blackout,

42:02.896 --> 42:07.246 align:middle
there was like an open
thought of 25 or 50-you know,

42:07.246 --> 42:09.276 align:middle
the Fed could move
either 25 or 50.

42:10.206 --> 42:13.836 align:middle
I would sort of argue that when
we had those two last speeches

42:13.896 --> 42:17.446 align:middle
by Governor Waller and New York
Fed President John Williams,

42:17.526 --> 42:19.286 align:middle
that they were sort of saying

42:19.286 --> 42:22.676 align:middle
that maybe a gradual approach
was going to win the day.

42:23.546 --> 42:26.546 align:middle
I mean, I sort of want to ask a
seven-part question about this.

42:26.546 --> 42:30.426 align:middle
But, I mean, could you
talk-would you have cut rates

42:30.466 --> 42:34.516 align:middle
by 50 basis points if the market
had been pricing in, like,

42:34.576 --> 42:37.996 align:middle
low odds of a 50 point move
like they were last Wednesday,

42:37.996 --> 42:39.636 align:middle
you know, after the
CPI number came out?

42:39.676 --> 42:43.066 align:middle
It was a really small
probability of a 50 point cut.

42:43.216 --> 42:46.986 align:middle
Does that play in your
consideration at all?

42:47.176 --> 42:48.176 align:middle
Just talk a little
bit about that.

42:48.276 --> 42:48.526 align:middle
Thank you.

42:48.816 --> 42:49.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:49.206 --> 42:52.296 align:middle
We're always going to try to do
what we think is the right thing

42:52.296 --> 42:53.596 align:middle
for the economy at that time.

42:54.046 --> 42:54.706 align:middle
That's what we'll do.

42:55.546 --> 42:56.386 align:middle
And that's what we did today.

42:56.386 --> 42:57.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:59.466 --> 43:01.796 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

43:02.786 --> 43:03.596 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

43:03.596 --> 43:05.586 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

43:05.836 --> 43:07.506 align:middle
You've mentioned how closely
you're watching the labor

43:07.506 --> 43:08.576 align:middle
market, but you also noted

43:08.576 --> 43:11.196 align:middle
that payroll numbers have been a
little bit less reliable lately

43:11.196 --> 43:12.736 align:middle
because of the big
downward revisions.

43:13.126 --> 43:15.296 align:middle
Does that put your
focus overwhelmingly

43:15.296 --> 43:16.386 align:middle
on the unemployment rate?

43:16.826 --> 43:18.386 align:middle
And given the SEP projection

43:18.386 --> 43:21.526 align:middle
of 4.4 [percent] basically
being the peak in the cycle,

43:21.996 --> 43:24.266 align:middle
would going above that
be the kind of thing

43:24.266 --> 43:27.016 align:middle
that would trigger
another 50 basis point cut?

43:27.066 --> 43:27.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:27.596 --> 43:30.706 align:middle
So we will continue to
look at that broad array

43:30.706 --> 43:32.996 align:middle
of labor market data,
including the payroll numbers.

43:32.996 --> 43:34.096 align:middle
We're not discarding those.

43:34.556 --> 43:37.456 align:middle
I mean, we'll certainly look at
those, but we will mentally tend

43:37.456 --> 43:40.716 align:middle
to adjust them based
on the QCEW adjustment,

43:40.716 --> 43:42.746 align:middle
which you referred to.

43:42.916 --> 43:44.046 align:middle
There isn't a bright line.

43:44.386 --> 43:47.626 align:middle
You know, it will be-the
unemployment rate's very

43:47.626 --> 43:50.756 align:middle
important, of course, but
there isn't a single statistic

43:50.756 --> 43:55.176 align:middle
or single bright line over
which that thing that might move

43:55.176 --> 43:57.376 align:middle
that would dictate
one thing or another.

43:57.376 --> 43:59.996 align:middle
We'll look at-each meeting,
we'll look at all the data

43:59.996 --> 44:03.296 align:middle
on inflation, economic
activity, and the labor market,

44:03.366 --> 44:06.586 align:middle
and we'll make decisions
about, is our policy stance

44:06.776 --> 44:09.616 align:middle
where it needs to be to foster

44:09.616 --> 44:12.286 align:middle
over the medium term
our mandate goals?

44:12.746 --> 44:16.646 align:middle
So I can't-I can't say we
have a bright line in mind.

44:18.106 --> 44:19.416 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:19.846 --> 44:20.156 align:middle
Matt Egan.

44:21.376 --> 44:21.736 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

44:21.876 --> 44:22.536 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

44:22.896 --> 44:24.106 align:middle
Matt Egan from CNN.

44:24.646 --> 44:27.766 align:middle
I know that you discussed
earlier how the Fed does

44:28.146 --> 44:31.426 align:middle
whatever the right thing is
and nothing else factors in.

44:31.616 --> 44:33.726 align:middle
But, in general, can
you talk about whether

44:33.726 --> 44:37.986 align:middle
or not you believe a sitting
U.S. president should have a say

44:38.256 --> 44:40.236 align:middle
in Fed decisions
on interest rates?

44:40.376 --> 44:42.126 align:middle
Because that's something
that former President Trump,

44:42.196 --> 44:45.646 align:middle
who obviously appointed you,
has previously suggested.

44:45.896 --> 44:49.696 align:middle
And I know the Fed is designed
to be independent, but why?

44:49.776 --> 44:52.606 align:middle
Can you tell the public why
you view that as so important?

44:53.026 --> 44:53.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:53.776 --> 44:58.066 align:middle
Sure. So countries
that are-democracies

44:58.066 --> 45:01.136 align:middle
around the world,
countries that are sort

45:01.136 --> 45:02.166 align:middle
of like the United States,

45:02.806 --> 45:05.156 align:middle
all have what are called
independent central banks.

45:05.156 --> 45:08.356 align:middle
And the reason is that
people have found over time

45:08.356 --> 45:12.526 align:middle
that insulating the central
bank from direct control

45:12.526 --> 45:18.666 align:middle
by political authorities avoids
making monetary policy in a way

45:18.756 --> 45:21.956 align:middle
that favors maybe people who are
in office as opposed to people

45:21.956 --> 45:22.866 align:middle
who are not in office.

45:23.166 --> 45:27.826 align:middle
So that's the idea, is that-you
know, I think the data are clear

45:27.826 --> 45:30.066 align:middle
that countries that have
independent central banks,

45:30.446 --> 45:31.706 align:middle
they get lower inflation.

45:31.946 --> 45:37.906 align:middle
And so we're, we're not-we do
our work to serve all Americans.

45:37.906 --> 45:39.516 align:middle
We're not serving
any politician,

45:39.746 --> 45:44.296 align:middle
any political figure, any
cause, any issue, nothing.

45:44.296 --> 45:46.886 align:middle
It's just maximum
employment and price stability

45:46.886 --> 45:48.276 align:middle
on behalf of all Americans.

45:48.656 --> 45:50.616 align:middle
And that's how the other
central banks are set up, too.

45:50.926 --> 45:53.256 align:middle
It's a good institutional
arrangement, which has been good

45:53.256 --> 45:57.676 align:middle
for the public and I hope-and
I hope and strongly believe

45:57.676 --> 45:58.976 align:middle
that it will continue.

45:59.296 --> 46:00.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:00.546 --> 46:01.746 align:middle
Kyle. KYLE CAMPBELL.

46:04.896 --> 46:06.406 align:middle
Kyle Campbell for
American Banker.

46:06.556 --> 46:08.186 align:middle
Thanks for taking the
questions, Chair Powell.

46:08.386 --> 46:11.066 align:middle
A couple regulatory
developments in the past week

46:11.116 --> 46:12.966 align:middle
that I want to ask you about.

46:12.966 --> 46:14.196 align:middle
First, last week Vice Chair

46:14.196 --> 46:17.176 align:middle
for Supervision Michael
Barr outlined his views

46:17.266 --> 46:19.046 align:middle
for the changes to
the Basel III endgame.

46:19.576 --> 46:22.896 align:middle
I'm wondering if you are
in alignment with him

46:22.896 --> 46:26.046 align:middle
on what those changes should
be, if those have support

46:26.426 --> 46:28.486 align:middle
on the Board in the broad
way that you're looking for,

46:28.976 --> 46:31.716 align:middle
and if you think the other
agencies are also fully onboard

46:31.716 --> 46:32.846 align:middle
with that approach.

46:33.416 --> 46:34.656 align:middle
And then yesterday,

46:34.816 --> 46:36.446 align:middle
other banking regulators-
CHAIR POWELL.

46:36.446 --> 46:37.596 align:middle
You know what, hold
the second question.

46:37.726 --> 46:38.316 align:middle
KYLE CAMPBELL.

46:38.316 --> 46:38.736 align:middle
Okay. Sure.

46:39.186 --> 46:40.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:40.576 --> 46:42.046 align:middle
We'll give you the
second question.

46:42.486 --> 46:48.406 align:middle
So the answer to your
question is that, yes, those,

46:48.456 --> 46:52.346 align:middle
those changes were
negotiated between the agencies

46:52.346 --> 46:55.676 align:middle
with my support and with my
involvement, with the idea

46:56.806 --> 46:59.446 align:middle
that we were going
to re-propose,

46:59.716 --> 47:05.036 align:middle
re-propose the changes that
Vice Chair Barr talked about

47:05.616 --> 47:06.926 align:middle
and then take comment on them.

47:07.536 --> 47:11.966 align:middle
So, yes, that is-that's
happening with my support.

47:11.966 --> 47:12.506 align:middle
KYLE CAMPBELL.

47:12.726 --> 47:14.226 align:middle
Is there a date for
that- CHAIR POWELL.

47:14.276 --> 47:16.056 align:middle
That's not a final proposal,
though, you understand.

47:16.056 --> 47:17.146 align:middle
We're putting them
out for comment.

47:17.146 --> 47:19.906 align:middle
We're going to take comment
and make appropriate changes.

47:20.226 --> 47:23.506 align:middle
We don't have a-we don't have
a calendar date for that, and,

47:23.506 --> 47:25.146 align:middle
as for the other
agencies, you know,

47:25.146 --> 47:27.236 align:middle
the idea is that we're
all moving together,

47:27.236 --> 47:28.346 align:middle
we're not moving separately,

47:28.346 --> 47:31.636 align:middle
so that-I don't know
exactly where that is.

47:31.686 --> 47:36.026 align:middle
But the idea is that we will
move as a group to put this,

47:36.026 --> 47:39.426 align:middle
again, out for comment,
and then, you know,

47:39.426 --> 47:42.076 align:middle
it'll-the comments will
come back 60 days later,

47:42.076 --> 47:45.196 align:middle
and we'll dive into them,
and we'll try to bring this

47:45.236 --> 47:47.666 align:middle
to a conclusion sometime in
the first half of next year.

47:48.096 --> 47:48.956 align:middle
KYLE CAMPBELL.

47:48.956 --> 47:49.996 align:middle
Right. And then, yesterday.

47:49.996 --> 47:53.146 align:middle
there were merger
reform finalizations

47:53.146 --> 47:54.396 align:middle
from the other bank regulators.

47:54.626 --> 47:57.476 align:middle
What does the Fed have to do
to align itself on a merger?

47:57.816 --> 47:58.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:58.326 --> 48:00.306 align:middle
You know, I would-I would bounce

48:00.306 --> 48:01.686 align:middle
that question to
Vice Chair Barr.

48:01.976 --> 48:04.446 align:middle
It's a good question, but
I don't have that today.

48:04.656 --> 48:06.076 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

48:06.076 --> 48:08.026 align:middle
Jennifer, for the last question.

48:08.076 --> 48:08.686 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

48:09.356 --> 48:10.256 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

48:10.256 --> 48:12.866 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

48:12.866 --> 48:14.736 align:middle
You said earlier that the
decision today reflects

48:14.866 --> 48:17.886 align:middle
with appropriate recalibration
strength in the labor market

48:17.886 --> 48:20.596 align:middle
that could be maintained in
the context of moderate growth,

48:21.436 --> 48:24.356 align:middle
even though the policy statement
says you view the risks

48:24.446 --> 48:27.696 align:middle
to inflation and job
growth as roughly balanced.

48:28.306 --> 48:30.586 align:middle
Given what you've said,
though, today, I'm curious,

48:30.836 --> 48:34.016 align:middle
are you more worried
about the job market

48:34.336 --> 48:37.816 align:middle
and growth than inflation?

48:38.006 --> 48:39.306 align:middle
Are they not roughly balanced?

48:39.426 --> 48:40.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:40.626 --> 48:42.236 align:middle
No, I think-I think,
and we think,

48:42.236 --> 48:43.406 align:middle
they are now roughly balanced.

48:43.406 --> 48:45.666 align:middle
So if you go back
for a long time,

48:46.296 --> 48:47.766 align:middle
the risks were on inflation.

48:47.766 --> 48:49.946 align:middle
We had a historically
tight labor market,

48:50.106 --> 48:50.936 align:middle
historically tight.

48:50.936 --> 48:52.446 align:middle
There was a severe
labor shortage,

48:53.036 --> 48:55.796 align:middle
so very hot labor market.

48:55.796 --> 48:57.586 align:middle
And we had inflation
way above target.

48:57.586 --> 49:01.016 align:middle
So, you know, that said to
us, concentrate on inflation,

49:01.016 --> 49:02.146 align:middle
concentrate on inflation.

49:02.146 --> 49:05.946 align:middle
And we did for a while, and we
kept at that, that the stance

49:05.996 --> 49:09.086 align:middle
that we put in place 14
months ago was a stance

49:09.086 --> 49:11.006 align:middle
that was focused on
bringing down inflation.

49:11.386 --> 49:12.716 align:middle
Part of bringing down
inflation, though,

49:12.716 --> 49:15.226 align:middle
is cooling off the economy

49:15.226 --> 49:16.866 align:middle
and a little bit cooling
off the labor market.

49:17.276 --> 49:19.086 align:middle
You now have a cooler
labor market,

49:19.086 --> 49:21.036 align:middle
in part because of our activity.

49:21.036 --> 49:24.376 align:middle
So what that tells you is,
it's time to change our stance.

49:24.376 --> 49:25.196 align:middle
So we did that.

49:25.716 --> 49:27.366 align:middle
The sense of the
change in the stance is

49:27.366 --> 49:31.206 align:middle
that we're recalibrating our
policy over time to a stance

49:31.206 --> 49:32.246 align:middle
that will be more neutral.

49:32.646 --> 49:36.056 align:middle
And today was-I think we made
a good strong start on that.

49:36.056 --> 49:37.916 align:middle
I think it was the
right decision,

49:38.386 --> 49:40.466 align:middle
and I think it should send
a signal that we, you know,

49:40.466 --> 49:43.426 align:middle
that we're committed to coming
up with a good outcome here.

49:43.426 --> 49:44.046 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

49:44.786 --> 49:47.556 align:middle
Is the economy more
vulnerable to a shock now

49:47.716 --> 49:48.906 align:middle
that could tip it
into recession?

49:48.906 --> 49:49.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:50.036 --> 49:50.816 align:middle
I don't think so.

49:50.976 --> 49:56.296 align:middle
I don't-there's-as I look-well,
let me look at it this way:

49:56.996 --> 50:00.826 align:middle
I don't see anything in the
economy right now that suggests

50:01.186 --> 50:04.136 align:middle
that the likelihood
of a recession-sorry,

50:04.136 --> 50:07.696 align:middle
of a downturn is elevated, okay?

50:07.796 --> 50:08.506 align:middle
I don't see that.

50:09.046 --> 50:11.506 align:middle
You see-you see growth
at a solid rate,

50:12.296 --> 50:13.566 align:middle
you see inflation coming down,

50:13.566 --> 50:14.836 align:middle
and you see a labor
market that's,

50:14.886 --> 50:17.076 align:middle
that's still at very
solid levels.

50:17.736 --> 50:19.636 align:middle
So, so I don't really
see that, no.

50:20.246 --> 50:20.576 align:middle
Thank you.

50:23.696 --> 50:33.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:37.256 --> 50:39.656 align:middle
Thank you.

